It's an interesting idea, and one that I've considered several times
myself (haven't we all?)

As Aardvark Joe points out, a "remake NetHack" project would have a
very long and unpleasant development leading to something we already
have.  I think that one solution to this is to make the project a
general roguelike framework, with a NetHack clone as a demo of what
can be built on the framework.  That'd allow the dev team to begin
with a smaller goal: implement Hack (and other smaller roguelikes) and
add functionality to the framework as necessary, moving towards
NetHack.

I'm a little concerned that people are suggesting C or C++ without any
reason beyond "that's what hackers like" or "I'm pretty good at it".
Java seems like a pretty reasonable choice because of its
portability.  Implementing a terminal with one of Java's GUI libs is
trivial, and Java's excessively-pedantic code style is not a terrible
thing for a big group project.

Of course, if we're just rooting for our favorite languages, Lisp does
a great job with multiple-inheritance, has the same portability
advantages as Java, but runs fast enough to satisfy hackers.


